schurModulesMap -- build a map between Schur modules from its action on tableaux
********************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        schurModulesMap(N, M, F)
  * Inputs:
      * N, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, a Schur module from *note
        schurModule: schurModule,
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, a Schur module from *note
        schurModule: schurModule,
      * F, a *note function: (Macaulay2Doc)Function,, a function describing the
        map on tableaux: for every SSYT T in the basis of M, F(T) should
        produce a list of pairs (c, T') where c is a coefficient in ring N and
        T' is a (not-necessarily-semistandard) filling of the shape of N
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, the $R$-linear map M -> N given,
        on each basis tableau T of M, by the $R$-linear combination $\sum (c,
        T') \in F(T)$, implicitly straightened into $N$

Description
===========

This constructor is the main tool for producing Schur-module homomorphisms from
combinatorial data.  It amounts to specifying where each tableau in a basis of
the source goes, in a possibly-non-standard form; the straightening algorithm
is invoked internally on each output to assemble the resulting vector in N.

Example: a piece of a Koszul-type differential.  For $\lambda = (1^j)$ and
$\lambda' = (1^{j+1})$ the natural map $\wedge^j(E) \to \wedge^{j+1}(E) \otimes
E^*$ sends a basis tableau to $\sum_k x_k \otimes (T \cup \{k\})$.  Lifting
this via schurModulesMap gives (a piece of) the Koszul differential:

+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : n = 4;                                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : j = 2;                                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = QQ[x_1..x_n];                                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : M = schurModule(toList(j : 1), R^n);                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : N = schurModule(toList(j+1 : 1), R^n);                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : F = T -> apply(numgens R, k -> (R_k, augmentFilling(T, 0, k)));|
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : schurModulesMap(N, M, F)                                       |
|                                                                    |
|o7 = | x_3 -x_2 x_1 0    0    0   |                                 |
|     | x_4 0    0   -x_2 x_1  0   |                                 |
|     | 0   x_4  0   -x_3 0    x_1 |                                 |
|     | 0   0    x_4 0    -x_3 x_2 |                                 |
|                                                                    |
|             4      6                                               |
|o7 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note schurModule: schurModule, -- Schur module of a partition applied to a
    free module
  * *note augmentFilling: augmentFilling, -- append an entry to a column of a
    Filling
  * *note straighten: straighten, -- straighten a filling into a linear
    combination of semistandard tableaux
  * *note weylModulesMap: weylModulesMap, -- build a map between Weyl modules
    from its action on tableaux

Ways to use schurModulesMap:
============================

  * "schurModulesMap(Module,Module,Function)"

