File: SchurFunctors.info, Node: weyl, Next: weylCharacter, Prev: towardWeylStandard, Up: Top

weyl -- construct a WeylFilling or apply the Weyl functor to a map
******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        weyl L
        weyl(lambda, f)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of rows, each row a list
        of integers (weakly increasing); interpreted as the rows of a
        WeylFilling
      * lambda, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a partition
      * f, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a map $f : E \to F$ between
        free modules
  * Outputs:
      * in the one-argument form, a WeylFilling; in the two-argument form, a
        Matrix giving the induced map $W_\lambda(f) : W_\lambda(E) \to
        W_\lambda(F)$

Description
===========

The one-argument form is a constructor for *note WeylFilling: WeylFilling,:

+------------------------------+
|i1 : T = weyl {{0,0,1}, {1,2}}|
|                              |
|     +-+-+-+                  |
|o1 = |0|0|1|                  |
|     |1|2| |                  |
|     +-+-+-+                  |
|                              |
|o1 : WeylFilling              |
+------------------------------+
|i2 : class T                  |
|                              |
|o2 = WeylFilling              |
|                              |
|o2 : Type                     |
+------------------------------+
|i3 : isWeylStandard T         |
+------------------------------+

The two-argument form is the Weyl functor applied to a morphism of free
modules.  Functoriality: $W_\lambda(f \circ g) = W_\lambda(f) \circ
W_\lambda(g)$ and $W_\lambda(\mathrm{id}) = \mathrm{id}$:

+--------------------------------+
|i4 : F = matrix{{1_QQ,2},{3,4}};|
|                                |
|              2       2         |
|o4 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ          |
+--------------------------------+
|i5 : weyl({2}, F)               |
|                                |
|o5 = | 1 4  4  |                |
|     | 3 10 8  |                |
|     | 9 24 16 |                |
|                                |
|              3       3         |
|o5 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ          |
+--------------------------------+
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : A = random(QQ^3, QQ^3);                              |
|                                                          |
|              3       3                                   |
|o6 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                    |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : B = random(QQ^3, QQ^3);                              |
|                                                          |
|              3       3                                   |
|o7 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                    |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : weyl({2,1}, A * B) == weyl({2,1}, A) * weyl({2,1}, B)|
|                                                          |
|o8 = true                                                 |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

For special shapes $W_\lambda$ coincides with familiar functors: $W_{(d)} =
D^d$ (divided power), $W_{(1^d)} = \wedge^d$ (exterior power, also equal to
$S_{(1^d)}$).

+---------------------------------------+
|i9 : weyl({2}, F) == dividedPower(2, F)|
|                                       |
|o9 = true                              |
+---------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note WeylFilling: WeylFilling, -- a Young tableau stored row-wise for the
    Weyl-module basis
  * *note weylModule: weylModule, -- Weyl module of a partition applied to a
    free module
